account.terms.n1turningitoffandhowquicklythattakeseffect2turn = { $slot-1 }Turning it off, and how quickly that takes effect.{ $slot-2 } Turn it off from the agents page in your journal, anytime. Your agents stop reaching it on their next request, because the check happens on your own device, not with us. Turning it off stops the { $slot-3 } relay carrying your traffic; it doesn't cancel a subscription, which you cancel with { $slot-4 }cancel contracts here{ $slot-5 } as in section 3.
account.terms.n1wekeepnoconnectionmetadataforthisservice2beyo = { $slot-1 }We keep no connection metadata for this service{ $slot-2 }, beyond the services portal's request trace, which for a credential check names no journal or sign-in. That's still a real difference from the private network and the operated backup. The privacy policy says what that trace holds, and what Azure's network and our providers' short-lived request logs still see.
account.terms.n1weneverselllicensesublicenseorleaseyourdataan = { $slot-1 }We never sell, license, sublicense, or lease your data, and we never use it for targeted advertising or behavioral profiling.{ $slot-2 } That isn't a policy. It's Article 8 of { $slot-3 }'s articles of incorporation, and it can't be amended without the founder's personal signature; after him, the language can only get stronger. You can read it at { $slot-4 }{ $slot-5 }{ $slot-6 }.
account.terms.n1wetellyoubeforeapricechanges2apricechangeisam = { $slot-1 }We tell you before a price changes.{ $slot-2 } A price change is a material change (section 9): we notify you first, and it applies only from a renewal, so you can cancel before it does.
account.terms.n1whataservicehandlestodoitsjob2isinitssectioni = { $slot-1 }What a service handles to do its job{ $slot-2 } is in its section in part two, and none of it is read by us except where a section says so in as many words. { $slot-3 }Your sign-in and billing details{ $slot-4 } are used only to run your sign-in and your subscriptions, and to keep our books. None of it is ever sold, licensed, shared for anyone else's purposes, profiled, used for advertising, or used to train any model.
account.terms.n1whateachagentmayseeisyourstosetandwerenotinit = { $slot-1 }What each agent may see is yours to set, and we're not in it.{ $slot-2 } When you connect an agent you choose what it may read: your whole journal, or only the parts of it you choose, and which of transcripts, entities, and facets it may reach within that. Transcripts means what was said in your recordings and imports, never the audio or the screen frames themselves; entities means the people, places, and projects your journal knows, and what it has noted about them; facets means facet names and descriptions, and the activities, events, and summaries filed in them. "Your whole journal" keeps including what you add later, facets you make later included. A facet you choose grows with its contents, though a facet you make later isn't in that choice unless you add it. { $slot-3 }It reads; it can't add, change, or delete anything.{ $slot-4 } You can narrow it or disconnect the agent whenever you like, and the change applies on that agent's next request, though what it already read stays with it; a request already in flight finishes under the old choice. It's your journal that checks, every time, not us. There are two ways to connect: pairing in your browser, or a key you create and paste in. { $slot-5 }A key is a bearer credential: anyone holding it can read what it may see until you revoke it{ $slot-6 }, so keep it as you would a password, and revoke it from the journal if it gets out. { $slot-7 }We hold no readable record of what any agent asked your journal for, what it was shown, or what it was refused.{ $slot-8 } Your journal keeps that record on your device, for you to read; it can have gaps, which it tells you about rather than hiding. We don't receive it in any form we can read, and we've built this so that it's never sent to us, except that if you use the operated backup, it's inside the encrypted copy of your journal, which we have no key to. Changing that would mean rebuilding the service, and we'd have to tell you first under section 9.
account.terms.n1whatitis2anaddressontheinternetforyourjournal = { $slot-1 }What it is.{ $slot-2 } An address on the internet for your journal, so an agent you already use can read from it: Claude, Codex, goose, or anything else that speaks the Model Context Protocol. You turn it on, you connect an agent, and you choose what that agent may see. Your journal makes itself reachable from wherever it is, and enforces that choice on your own device. We run the { $slot-3 } relay in between, so your agent can find your journal without you running anything of your own. It's off until you turn it on, and you can turn it off from the journal at any time.
account.terms.n1whatitis2anaimodelsolpbcrunsonconfidentialgpu = { $slot-1 }What it is.{ $slot-2 } An AI model { $slot-3 } runs on confidential GPU hardware, so your journal can think with more capacity (room to think faster and longer) than the device it lives on. It's off until you turn it on, and you can turn it off from the journal at any time.
account.terms.n1whatitis2arelaysolpbcrunssoyourdevicescanreac = { $slot-1 }What it is.{ $slot-2 } A relay { $slot-3 } runs so your devices can reach your journal from anywhere, without you running a relay of your own.
account.terms.n1whatitis2notificationsarebuiltintothesolstone = { $slot-1 }What it is.{ $slot-2 } Notifications are built into the solstone app: free, turned on by you for each device, no sign-in needed. They give you a short heads-up on your devices when there's something worth a look.
account.terms.n1whatitis2storagesolpbcrunssoanencryptedcopyof = { $slot-1 }What it is.{ $slot-2 } Storage { $slot-3 } runs so an encrypted copy of your journal can live somewhere other than your own machine, without you setting up a bucket (a bucket is what cloud storage calls a folder you rent).
account.terms.n1whatsuspensioncantouchandwhatitnevercan2susta = { $slot-1 }What suspension can touch, and what it never can.{ $slot-2 } Sustained abuse can suspend your access to a service. It can never suspend your journal, your devices, or any path of your own: those are yours and don't run through us. If we suspend access we tell you why and what it would take to restore it, and you can reply at { $slot-3 }
account.terms.n1whatthemodelproducesisnotadviceandnobodycheck = { $slot-1 }What the model produces is not advice, and nobody checks it.{ $slot-2 } A model can be confidently wrong. Don't rely on what it produces for medical, legal, financial, safety, or any other decision that matters, and check it before you act. As between you and { $slot-3 }, { $slot-4 }what you send and what comes back are yours.{ $slot-5 } You grant { $slot-6 } a limited license to process what you send, for as long as it takes to answer, solely to run confidential processing and hand the result back to you, and for nothing else. We claim no ownership of either, and make no warranty that output is accurate, complete, current, or fit for any purpose.
account.terms.n1whattherelayhandlesandwhatwekeep2tomoveyourby = { $slot-1 }What the relay handles, and what we keep.{ $slot-2 } To move your bytes, the relay and { $slot-3 }, whose network it runs on, necessarily handle connection metadata, and pairing a device issues that device a credential, which the device holds. { $slot-4 }The relay keeps no list of your devices{ $slot-5 }, and no connection log of its own. What we keep is a record of your journal, and two access records at { $slot-6 }. The privacy policy lists each one, what it holds, and how long ({ $slot-7 }private network{ $slot-8 }). The records we keep are Customer Data under our covenants, used only to operate and secure the relay, and deleted with your sign-in, apart from what section 7 says outlasts a deletion. { $slot-9 }The relay never reads, stores, or analyzes what's inside your traffic.{ $slot-10 } Your traffic passes through and is gone.
account.terms.n1whattheservicesworkwith2eachserviceworkswitht = { $slot-1 }What the services work with.{ $slot-2 } Each service works with the solstone software on your own devices, on the systems listed at { $slot-3 }, and each section below says what the service does and anything it needs.
account.terms.n1whatthestoragehandlesandwhatwekeep2theencrypt = { $slot-1 }What the storage handles, and what we keep.{ $slot-2 } The encrypted blocks; a small amount of operational information about them (how many, how much space, when they last changed); the connection metadata of your upload sessions, which the storage ({ $slot-3 } R2) necessarily handles; and a few access and credential records at { $slot-4 }. None of it says anything about your content. The privacy policy lists each record, what it holds, and how long ({ $slot-5 }encrypted backup{ $slot-6 }). The encrypted blocks and the records we keep are Customer Data under our covenants, used only to operate and secure the service, and deleted when your sign-in is, apart from what section 7 says outlasts a deletion.
account.terms.n1whatthisrelayandourcontrolplanehandleandwhatw = { $slot-1 }What this relay and our control plane handle, and what we keep.{ $slot-2 } To carry your traffic, the { $slot-3 } relay and the Azure network it runs on necessarily handle connection metadata. We see that, and never what was said, and the relay keeps no record of it. What we do keep is the link between your address and your journal, the record that you confirmed the turn-on screen, and the no-name reservation of the address. { $slot-4 }We keep no record of the passes your journal asks us for{ $slot-5 }, beyond a trace of each request, which carries nothing that ties it to you and expires on its own. The privacy policy lists each record, what { $slot-6 } sees when your address is minted and looked up, how long anything lasts, and what { $slot-7 } and { $slot-8 }, which control the { $slot-9 } name, could do ({ $slot-10 }{ $slot-11 }{ $slot-12 }). All of it is Customer Data under our covenants, used only to operate and secure this service. The binding is deleted with your sign-in; what outlasts that is the no-name address reservation above, the public certificate records, and what section 7 says outlasts a deletion.
