account.terms.thesetermsareacontractbetweenyouand1solpbc2aco = These terms are a contract between you and { $slot-1 }{ $slot-2 }{ $slot-3 }, a Colorado public benefit corporation. They cover:
account.terms.thesetermsarebetweenyouand1solpbc2acoloradopub = These terms are between you and { $slot-1 }{ $slot-2 }{ $slot-3 }, a Colorado public benefit corporation, and they're governed by Colorado law. { $slot-4 }If you're a consumer, you also keep the protections of the law where you live that an agreement can't take away.{ $slot-5 }
account.terms.thesetermscover1servicessolstoneapp2theplaceyo = These terms cover { $slot-1 }{ $slot-2 }{ $slot-3 }, the place you sign in, and the { $slot-4 }solstone services{ $slot-5 } { $slot-6 } runs for you there: private network, encrypted backup, confidential processing, { $slot-7 }, and the part of notifications { $slot-8 } runs. They're between you and { $slot-9 }. The short version comes first; the details follow.
account.terms.thesetermsdescribeservicessolpbcruns = These terms describe the services { $slot-1 } runs and the covenants that bind them. The covenants come first.
account.terms.thesetermsprivacypolicydisclosureyoure = These terms, the privacy policy, the disclosure you're shown when you turn on a service or a feature of one, the plan terms you're shown at checkout, and, if you're a scout, the program disclosure you acknowledged, are the whole agreement between us about the services. If these terms ever conflict with { $slot-1 }'s articles of incorporation, the articles govern.
account.terms.theydontcoversolstonesoftwareitself = They don't cover the solstone software itself, which is open source under its own license, or anything you run on your own devices, your own network, or your own storage. We aren't in the path for any of that, and these terms don't reach it.
account.terms.thissectionappliesanyserviceyousubscribe = This section applies to any service you subscribe to: today, private network, the operated tier of encrypted backup, confidential processing, and { $slot-1 }. Prices are in the services portal and at checkout. If you have two journals, that's two subscriptions.
account.terms.twootherdocumentsgowiththesetermsthe1privacypo = Two other documents go with these terms. The { $slot-1 }privacy policy{ $slot-2 } at { $slot-3 }{ $slot-4 }{ $slot-5 } is part of this agreement, and it is the one home for what { $slot-6 } holds: what each service handles and keeps, who processes it, how long it lasts, how to see, export and delete it, and what outlasts a deletion. These terms are the one home for what each service does, and for paying, canceling, refunds and the legal limits. Where one document needs the other's facts it points there rather than repeating them. And when you turn on a service, the page you turn it on from tells you what that particular service does.
account.terms.undecretfixenotammentlesmodalitestechniquesden = Un décret fixe notamment les modalités techniques de nature à garantir une identification du consommateur et un accès facile, direct et permanent à la fonctionnalité mentionnée au deuxième alinéa, telles que ses modalités de présentation et d'utilisation. Il détermine les informations devant être fournies par le consommateur.
account.terms.wecanhandthesetermsoryour = We can hand these terms, or your data, to another company only in the way Article 8 allows (section 6).
account.terms.wedonttakeorstoreyourcardpaymentsrunthrough1st = We don't take or store your card. Payments run through { $slot-1 }{ $slot-2 }{ $slot-3 }, our payment processor. Paying by card is your choice, and choosing it is what sends your card, the name on it and your billing address to { $slot-4 }, which handles them under its own { $slot-5 }terms{ $slot-6 } and { $slot-7 }privacy policy{ $slot-8 }. { $slot-9 } may offer you its own Link wallet at our checkout; it's { $slot-10 }'s product under { $slot-11 }'s terms, it's optional, and using it changes nothing about what we keep.
account.terms.wemayupdatethesetermsifchange = We may update these terms. If a change is material, we'll notify you before it takes effect, in a form you can keep, with how to cancel (C.R.S. § 6-1-732(3)). For a change that takes effect at your next renewal, you can cancel before then if you don't agree. If a material change has to take effect mid-term, the notice says how to cancel for a prorated refund of the unused period (today: write to { $slot-1 } before the date). If you keep using a service after a change takes effect, that's how you accept it; if you don't want to, turn the service off or close your sign-in before then.
account.terms.weworkkeepeveryserviceupbut = We work to keep every service up, but we don't guarantee uninterrupted service. A service can go down for maintenance or for reasons outside our control, and your journal won't use confidential processing when it can't verify it. A path of your own is always your fallback, and your journal on your devices is never on the line.
account.terms.whatwesendstripewhatwekeepfromwhatitsendsbacka = What we send { $slot-1 }, what we keep from what it sends back, and what we can see at { $slot-2 } are in the privacy policy ({ $slot-3 }billing{ $slot-4 }). { $slot-5 }We never send { $slot-6 } anything from your journal.{ $slot-7 }
account.terms.wheneverconfidentialprocessingisinuseandthepag = { $slot-1 }whenever confidential processing is in use, and the page where you turn confidential processing on says so. Turn it off and speech becomes text on your own device instead, once any transcription already under way finishes.
account.terms.whensectionsaysweemailyouwe = When a section says we email you, we use the primary address on your sign-in; keep it current.
account.terms.whentheaudioswitchisonyourjournalsendsspeechfo = { $slot-1 }When the audio switch is on, your journal sends speech for transcription over the same verified channel. It's served with{ $slot-2 }
account.terms.whenweaddserviceweaddits = When we add a service, we add its section here. If we ever retire a service you've paid for, we refund the time you paid for past the date it stops. If { $slot-1 } ever winds down, Article 8 still governs where your data can go: anyone it goes to must take on covenants no less protective (section 6). We keep the current version posted here with the date it took effect.
account.terms.youagreethesetermsfirsttimeyou = You agree to these terms the first time you do any of these: sign in to { $slot-1 }, turn on a service, or subscribe to one at checkout. Each of those pages says so and links here, so you can read them first. If you already have a sign-in, signing in, renewing, or keeping a service on is how you agree to this version; if you don't agree, turn the service off or cancel. Section 9 says what happens if you reject a later material change. If you're new and don't agree, don't sign in and don't turn anything on; solstone keeps working without us.
account.terms.youcanalsodeleteoperatedcopyyourself = You can also delete the operated copy yourself, anytime, from the backup screen in your journal; and closing your sign-in deletes it (section 7), with no 30-day lapse window.
account.terms.youhaveprivacyrightsyourstateor = You have the privacy rights your state or country gives you, and we extend them to everyone, wherever you live. Exercise any of them at { $slot-1 }
account.terms.youneedbeatleast13sign = You need to be at least 13 to sign in, and at least 18 (or the age of majority where you live) to subscribe to a paid service. Our services aren't directed to children under 13, and if we learn a sign-in belongs to someone under 13, we delete it.
account.terms.yourencryptedblocksthenstayinour = Your encrypted blocks then stay in our storage for 30 days. Re-subscribe within those 30 days and the operated tier turns back on against your existing backup with nothing lost.
account.terms.yourjournalisalwaysprivateonlyyoursand1nothing = Your journal is always private, only yours, and { $slot-1 }nothing { $slot-2 } runs reads a word of it except where a section below says so.{ $slot-3 } Private network carries encrypted bytes it can't read. Encrypted backup holds encrypted blocks we have no key to. Of the services, { $slot-4 } is the one that lets an agent you choose read from it, and that agent is never us: it gives your journal an address so that agent can read the part of it you allow, over a connection our relay passes along and cannot read. With confidential processing, our own model reads what you send, in the clear, only while it answers you, and verifiably keeps none of it; while it's on, your speech goes for transcription too, unless you turn that switch off. Each service's section says what { $slot-5 }, and the provider whose hardware it runs on, can see.
